        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<BR>
Acoustic data from the International Space Station (ISS) indicate that there is a significant risk to crew members' hearing. However, no method currently exists to monitor the hearing acuity of ISS crews. The objective of this project is to provide a means for reliable hearing assessment of ISS crews over extended space missions. 
<P>
APPROACH:<BR>
This experiment proposed an innovative design for digital active noise reduction in functional audiometer headsets (Creare headset system). The noise reduction system was designed to attenuate background noise and thus permit hearing assessment tests in space and relatively high noise environments on Earth. The research efforts included laboratory-scale technology development and assessment, clinical trials of the device, and design of a space qualified instrument.
<P>
RESULTS:<BR>
The following activities were performed: obtained and analyzed noise measured within the International Space Station (ISS) service module; characterized the performance of passive designs and materials used in noise reduction headsets; quantified the performance of both passive and active commercial-off-the-shelf (COTS) noise reducing headsets in the presence of ISS noise; measured the performance of a preliminary bench top version of a feedforward active noise reduction algorithm in ISS noise; performed an initial clinical evaluation of hearing assessment methods with passive hearing protection in noisy environments; supported evaluation of the passive hardware by NASA personnel; fabricated and tested the performance of a custom passive noise-reducing headset in combination with otoacoustic hearing evaluations; and began development of bench top hardware for testing feedforward active noise reducing headsets for this application.
<P>
Distortion product otoacoustic emision (DPOAE) testing can be a reliable way to assess hearing during long duration stays on the ISS. Threshold hearing testing using Békésy audiometry may also be feasible. Furthermore, the Creare headset system should enable hearing assessment tests to be administered in less controlled Earth environments than is currently possible. This technology can be employed by all DoD and civilian facilities where hearing tests are performed, such as field hospitals, physicians offices, occupational health clinics, and grade schools.
        </div>

